{
  "summary": "The change improves scheme validation, canonical self-origin checks, expiration, and per-instance creation throttling, but several reliability and abuse-resistance gaps remain. The most significant issues are unbounded in-memory rate-limiter state, unrestricted short-code probing, missing HTTP request-size limits, and a development-style localhost fallback for public URL construction.",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "MEDIUM",
      "file": "src/main/java/com/example/shortener/service/CreationRateLimiter.java",
      "issue": "The per-IP window map grows without eviction. Every distinct remote address creates a permanent ConcurrentHashMap entry, so an attacker able to generate many distinct source addresses can exhaust heap memory even though each individual IP remains within the 30-per-minute limit.",
      "recommendation": "Bound and expire limiter keys, for example with scheduled cleanup, an inactivity TTL, or a bounded cache. Preserve the explicitly accepted per-application-instance behavior while preventing unbounded key retention."
    },
    {
      "severity": "MEDIUM",
      "file": "src/main/java/com/example/shortener/controller/RedirectController.java",
      "issue": "Unauthenticated redirect requests have no rate limiting or abuse control. The response differs for valid and missing seven-character codes, providing an enumeration oracle. Although the 62^7 search space is large, automated probing remains possible and can expose links if codes are leaked or the code space is reduced.",
      "recommendation": "Add read-side abuse controls such as per-client/IP throttling, response monitoring, and optionally a uniform failure strategy. Do not rely solely on the short-code space for protection."
    },
    {
      "severity": "MEDIUM",
      "file": "src/main/resources/application.yml",
      "issue": "When APP_PUBLIC_ORIGIN is absent, app.public-origin silently defaults to http://localhost:${server.port}. In a deployed environment this can produce unusable or misleading short URLs rather than failing startup when no real public origin is configured.",
      "recommendation": "Require APP_PUBLIC_ORIGIN or an explicitly documented, validated server-origin property in production profiles. Fail startup when the effective origin is the localhost development default unless that default is explicitly enabled for local development."
    },
    {
      "severity": "MEDIUM",
      "file": "src/main/java/com/example/shortener/controller/LinkController.java",
      "issue": "There is no configured maximum HTTP request-body size. The URL field is limited only after JSON parsing and request binding, so a client can send very large JSON bodies or unknown fields and consume parsing memory and bandwidth before validation rejects the request.",
      "recommendation": "Configure web-server maximum request/header/body sizes and, where appropriate, reject unknown or excessively large JSON content before application-level validation."
    },
    {
      "severity": "LOW",
      "file": "src/main/java/com/example/shortener/service/AuditService.java",
      "issue": "recordFailure catches and suppresses all audit persistence failures. Consequently, rate-limit rejections and expired-link accesses can return their intended HTTP responses while producing neither the required persisted audit event nor a reliable operational signal that auditing failed.",
      "recommendation": "Use a durable asynchronous audit mechanism or an operationally monitored failure queue. At minimum, emit a structured, alertable log/metric when persistence fails and define whether the request should fail closed for required audit events."
    },
    {
      "severity": "MEDIUM",
      "file": "src/main/java/com/example/shortener/service/UrlValidator.java",
      "issue": "The service intentionally permits every syntactically valid external HTTP/HTTPS destination, and RedirectController returns that destination in the Location header. This is an open-redirect-like capability inherent in the public shortener and can be abused for phishing or reputation attacks.",
      "recommendation": "accepted risk: the binding decision explicitly keeps malicious-URL scanning and destination reputation/private-network blocking out of scope. If the threat model changes, add destination policy controls, reputation checks, or an allow-list before issuing redirects."
    }
  ],
  "decisions": [
    {
      "title": "Treat unrestricted destination redirects as an accepted risk rather than a release blocker",
      "rationale": "The human binding decision explicitly excludes malicious-URL scanning, reputation checks, and private-network blocking. The behavior is still recorded because arbitrary stored destinations are returned as redirects.",
      "alternatives": [
        "Block private, loopback, link-local, and suspicious destinations",
        "Add reputation scanning or an administrator-managed destination allow-list"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Do not report direct request-Host usage in production URL construction",
      "rationale": "The production dependency path uses PublicOriginProperties. The request-derived path exists only in the compatibility constructor for direct unit-test callers, while the human decision explicitly requires configured-origin construction in the service runtime.",
      "alternatives": [
        "Remove the compatibility constructor entirely",
        "Make the compatibility constructor reject blank configuration instead of deriving a request origin"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Classify short-code enumeration as MEDIUM",
      "rationale": "The seven-character random code provides a large search space, but unauthenticated valid-versus-missing responses still create an enumeration oracle and there is no redirect-side throttling.",
      "alternatives": [
        "Classify as LOW because of the current brute-force cost",
        "Classify as HIGH if links are considered sensitive and code disclosure is a primary threat"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Treat per-instance rate limiting as compliant with the stated decision",
      "rationale": "The human decision explicitly accepts a rate limiter scoped to each application instance and specifies the direct remote address as the client identity.",
      "alternatives": [
        "Use a shared datastore for cluster-wide enforcement",
        "Trust proxy forwarding headers when deployed behind a load balancer"
      ]
    }
  ]
